You may also see:

Protocol        : UDP

or:

Status          : LISTEN

or:

Destination IP  : N/A
Destination Port: N/A

Those are all possible and don't automatically indicate an attack.

Why we're doing this

ACTDRS is gradually building a network event like:

┌──────────────────────────────┐
│       NETWORK EVENT          │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ Process: firefox             │
│ PID: 1234                    │
│ Protocol: TCP                │
│ Status: ESTABLISHED          │
│ Source IP: 192.168.1.5       │
│ Source Port: 52341           │
│ Destination IP: 142.x.x.x    │
│ Destination Port: 443        │
└──────────────────────────────┘

This is much more useful for a SOC than simply recording:

CPU = 95%
⚠️ One important correction

Don't call this AI threat detection yet.

At this stage ACTDRS is doing:

Collection → Identification → Classification

It is not yet determining whether a network connection is malicious.

Later we'll add rules such as:

Unusual destination
        +
Unusual port
        +
Unexpected process
        +
Repeated connections
        ↓
Suspicious network behavior

Then that information can feed your decision engine.

